Held back from the branch bot's push: the bot token has no `workflows`
scope, and GitHub rejects any push that touches a file under
.github/workflows/ without it (the same reason docs/ci-workflow-comment.patch
exists). Apply with a token that has it:

    git checkout main && git pull
    git apply docs/ci-workflow-audiofit.patch
    git add .github/workflows/ci.yml
    git commit -m "CI: byte-compile audio_standardizer.py in the syntax gate"
    git push

What it is: one line in .github/workflows/ci.yml adding audio_standardizer.py
to the byte-compile (syntax) gate, so the new tool is compiled in CI exactly
like the other seven scripts. CI behaves identically before and after; the
gate simply didn't know the new file exists until this lands.
diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
index f0728f5..4ef2a6e 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml
@@ -45,7 +45,7 @@ jobs:
       - name: Byte-compile the package and every script (syntax gate)
         run: >-
           python -m compileall -q organizekit
-          organize.py pipeline.py bitdepth.py
+          organize.py pipeline.py audio_standardizer.py bitdepth.py
           library_auditor.py mkv_track_cleaner.py movie_standardizer.py
           subtitle_extractor.py
 
